.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt SPLICE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm splice ,
.Nm tee ,
.Nm sendfile
.Nd copy between descriptors, which this kernel leaves to read and write
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn splice "int fd_in" "off_t *off_in" "int fd_out" "off_t *off_out" "size_t len" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn tee "int fd_in" "int fd_out" "size_t len" "unsigned int flags"
.In sys/sendfile.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn sendfile "int out_fd" "int in_fd" "off_t *offset" "size_t count"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On Linux these move data from one descriptor to another without copying it
through the caller.
This kernel moves no data between two descriptors itself: once both
descriptors are open, each call fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
as Linux fails for descriptors it cannot splice or send from, and the
caller copies through
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
instead.
GNU grep, for one, drains a pipe it has read enough of with
.Fn splice
when its output is
.Pa /dev/null ,
and reads the pipe after an
.Er EINVAL ,
which it takes for a descriptor of the wrong kind, where any other error
would be its read's.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 313 for
.Fn splice ,
with the descriptors in EBX and EDX, 315 for
.Fn tee ,
and 187 for
.Fn sendfile
and 239 for
.Fn sendfile64 ,
with the output descriptor in EBX and the input in ECX.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each call returns -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
calls
.Fn splice
from a pipe to
.Pa /dev/null ,
.Fn tee
and
.Fn sendfile ,
and requires
.Er EINVAL
of each and
.Er EBADF
of a closed descriptor.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
One of the descriptors is not open.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Both descriptors are open; the kernel copies between none.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2
